"""Authored and explained Y9 electricity questions; independent revision, never lesson telemetry."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
STATIC={
'Atoms and charge':'''Which particle has negative electric charge?|An electron.|A proton.|A neutron.|An entire neutral nucleus.|Electrons are negative. Protons are positive and neutrons have no electric charge.
Where are protons found in an atom?|In its nucleus.|Only in wires outside the atom.|In a separate cloud of negative electrons.|Only on the outside of a charged balloon.|The nucleus is the central region containing protons and neutrons. Electrons occupy the surrounding region.
What does electrically neutral mean?|Positive and negative charges balance to give zero net charge.|There are no electrons or protons.|Every particle is a neutron.|The object cannot contain atoms.|Neutral matter contains charged particles, but their charges cancel overall.
Which is the correct unit symbol for electric charge?|C.|A.|V.|Ω.|Charge is measured in coulombs, symbol C. A is ampere, V volt and Ω ohm.
A selected patch has 8 protons and 9 electrons. What is its net charge?|−1 elementary charge.|+1 elementary charge.|Zero.|−17 elementary charges.|Eight positive and nine negative equal-sized elementary charges give 8 − 9 = −1.
A selected patch has 8 protons and 6 electrons. What is its net charge?|+2 elementary charges.|−2 elementary charges.|+14 elementary charges.|Zero.|Its positive total exceeds its negative total by two elementary charges: 8 − 6 = +2.
Which particle has no electric charge?|A neutron.|A proton.|An electron.|A positively charged ion.|A neutron is electrically neutral. An ion is an atom or group of atoms with net charge.
How many coulombs is 1 μC?|0.000001 C.|1000000 C.|0.001 C.|1 C.|Micro means one millionth: 1 μC = 10⁻⁶ C. The prefix is different from milli, which is one thousandth.
Approximately how many excess electrons give a charge of −1 C?|6.24 × 10¹⁸.|6.24.|1 × 10⁻¹⁹.|6.24 × 10⁻¹⁸.|The magnitude of one electron’s charge is about 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. Divide 1 C by this magnitude to obtain about 6.24 × 10¹⁸ electrons.
Why can a neutral metal still conduct charge?|It has mobile electrons, despite having zero net charge overall.|Neutral means it has no particles.|Only net-positive objects can conduct.|All its protons move freely between atoms.|Conductivity depends on mobile carriers, not a pre-existing net charge. The positive ion cores and electrons can balance while some electrons remain mobile.''',
'Electron transfer and conservation':'''In the chosen balloon–cloth model, which particles transfer to the balloon?|Electrons.|Protons.|Whole nuclei.|Neutrons only.|The model transfers electrons from cloth to balloon; the positive nuclei stay in their materials.
What happens to a neutral balloon when it gains electrons?|It becomes negatively charged.|It becomes positively charged.|It loses all its protons.|Its net charge must stay zero.|Adding negative charge produces a negative imbalance if the positive total stays unchanged.
What happens to the cloth when it loses electrons to the balloon?|It becomes positively charged.|It becomes negatively charged.|Its protons all disappear.|It becomes a pure electron gas.|Losing negative electrons leaves a positive excess. Positive charge does not require protons to move into the cloth.
What does conservation of charge mean for an isolated pair?|Its total charge stays the same.|Neither object can ever change charge.|Rubbing creates equal amounts of new matter.|Only the negative total is conserved.|Charge can transfer between the objects while the total of the isolated system stays unchanged.
Two initially neutral patches exchange three electrons from cloth to balloon. What are their net charges?|Balloon −3 e; cloth +3 e.|Balloon +3 e; cloth −3 e.|Both −3 e.|Both zero.|Three negative elementary charges leave one patch and enter the other. The total is still zero.
Which statement about rubbing is correct?|Contact and separation can transfer existing electrons.|Friction manufactures new electrons from energy in every case.|Protons always move from cloth into plastic.|Every rubbed material must become negative.|The transferred electrons already exist. The direction depends on the material pair and surface conditions.
What does static mean in static electricity?|A charge imbalance persists without a continuous circuit current.|Electrons can never move at any stage.|There is no electric force.|Only positively charged matter is present.|Static charge can move while an object is charging, redistributing charge or discharging. Static does not mean charge is permanently immobile.
An isolated pair starts with total charge zero. One object finishes at +2 μC. What is the other object’s charge?|−2 μC.|+2 μC.|Zero.|−4 μC.|Conservation requires the two final charges to add to the initial zero total.
Why is an object positive after electron loss, even though no proton entered?|Its unchanged positive charge now exceeds its negative charge.|Electrons changed into protons.|Losing matter always produces positive charge.|Positive charge is made by rubbing air.|Net charge is the signed sum. Removing negative charge leaves an imbalance in favour of the positive total already present.
An isolated pair initially totals +1 μC. After contact, one object is −2 μC. What is the other?|+3 μC.|−3 μC.|+1 μC.|Zero.|The total remains +1 μC. The other charge must satisfy −2 + Q = +1, so Q = +3 μC.''',
'Forces and polarisation':'''How do two negative net charges interact?|They repel.|They always attract.|They cancel without contact.|They exert no force because both are negative.|Like net charges repel: negative–negative and positive–positive.
How do opposite net charges interact?|They attract.|They repel.|They always become neutral without transfer.|They cannot interact across a gap.|Opposite charge signs attract. A force can act without direct contact.
What is an electrical force?|A push or pull associated with charge.|A quantity measured only in coulombs per second.|A kind of chemical fuel.|The number of neutrons in an atom.|Force is a push or pull. Electrical forces are associated with charge and can act across a space.
What does polarisation mean here?|A small redistribution of charge within an object.|Turning every neutron into an electron.|Destroying all positive charge.|Requiring the object to acquire a non-zero net charge.|Charges shift slightly so opposite charge can be nearer an external charged object; the polarised object can remain neutral overall.
Why can a negative balloon attract a neutral wall?|The wall’s charge distribution shifts, with positive charge effectively nearer.|The wall must always have a negative net charge.|Gravity reverses near balloons.|The balloon sends new protons through the wall.|Polarisation puts opposite charge closer. That attraction can outweigh the more distant like-charge repulsion.
Does attraction prove that a nearby object has the opposite net charge?|No; a neutral object can be polarised and attracted.|Yes, in every possible case.|Yes, because neutral objects have no charges inside.|No, because charges never attract.|Both an oppositely charged object and a neutral polarised object can be attracted.
Which observation best supports a like net charge in this introductory rod test?|Repulsion from a rod with known charge.|Attraction to a known charged rod alone.|The rod being the same colour.|The rod having greater mass.|Repulsion provides stronger evidence of like charge. Attraction alone can also occur through polarisation of neutral matter.
For unchanged point charges in air, what generally happens as separation increases?|The magnitude of electric force decreases.|The charge signs automatically reverse.|Electrons turn into neutrons.|The force always grows.|For the point-charge idealisation, force magnitude is proportional to 1/r². The force arrows in our introductory lab are qualitative.
A neutral object is polarised beside a charged one, with no charge transferred. What is its total charge?|Still zero.|Always positive.|Always negative.|Equal to the charged object’s charge.|Redistribution changes position, not the signed total, when no charge enters or leaves.
Two like charged rods are moved further apart. Which statement is consistent?|They still repel, usually with a weaker force.|They must begin attracting at any larger distance.|Each rod instantly becomes neutral.|Their protons leave through the air.|Changing distance changes force magnitude without necessarily changing net charge or whether the interaction attracts or repels.''',
'Conductors and insulators':'''What makes a metal a good conductor?|It has mobile charge carriers, including electrons.|Its protons travel around a circuit freely.|It contains no charges at all.|It is always positively charged.|Some electrons in a metal can move through it; the positive ion cores remain part of the lattice.
Why can charge persist on a dry plastic comb?|Plastic does not let charge move through it easily.|Plastic has no atoms.|Plastic makes new charge continuously.|Its protons are moving faster than light.|An insulator does not readily conduct charge through its bulk, so a surface imbalance can persist.
Which is normally a good electrical conductor?|Copper.|Dry polythene.|Dry glass.|Dry rubber.|Copper has mobile electrons. These dry insulating materials do not conduct nearly as readily under ordinary conditions.
What is a lattice in this lesson?|An organised arrangement of particles in a solid.|A unit of current.|A single electron in empty space.|An electrical energy unit.|Metal ion cores occupy positions in a solid lattice and vibrate around those positions.
What usually moves through a metal earth wire?|Electrons.|Protons migrating from nucleus to nucleus.|Whole balloon molecules.|Only neutrons.|Electronic conduction in metal involves mobile electrons; positive ion cores do not travel along the wire.
Can a neutral copper wire carry current?|Yes, its mobile electrons can drift while it stays nearly neutral overall.|No, it must first lose all electrons.|No, only charged insulators conduct.|Yes, because its protons flow through the circuit.|A wire can have balanced overall charge and still support a directed flow of mobile electrons.
Why might humid conditions reduce some static effects?|Moisture can provide paths for charge to leak away.|Water destroys all electrons.|Humidity turns all matter into a perfect conductor.|Protons stop having charge in damp air.|The leakage depends on surface and environmental conditions. The point is a possible discharge path, not disappearance of charge.
Why does touching one patch of a charged insulator with an earth lead not guarantee complete discharge?|Charge cannot readily move from all other patches to that contact.|The Earth cannot ever accept electrons.|The wire’s protons must first become electrons.|Insulators cannot contain net charge.|Local contact may discharge a patch, but poor bulk and surface conduction can keep remote charge in place.
Which comparison separates conductivity from net charge?|A neutral metal can conduct while a charged plastic surface can retain local charge.|Every charged object is automatically a good conductor.|Only metals contain electrons.|An insulator must be neutral at all times.|Net charge is an imbalance; conductivity describes mobility. They are different properties.
Why are the positive ion cores not shown circulating with electrons in a metal?|They remain bound in the lattice, although they vibrate.|Their positive charge has no force.|They disappear as soon as current starts.|Every metal nucleus is actually neutral.|The metal’s structure holds the ion cores in the solid. Mobile electrons carry the net drift current.''',
'Earthing and discharge':'''What is earthing a metal object?|Connecting it to Earth by a conducting path.|Adding an insulating stand only.|Rubbing it until protons leave.|Removing every electron permanently.|An earth connection allows charge transfer between the conducting object and the large charge reservoir of Earth.
A negative metal sphere is earthed with no nearby charging object. Which way do excess electrons move?|From sphere to Earth.|From Earth to sphere only.|From the sphere’s nuclei into new protons.|Nowhere, because metals are insulators.|The sphere’s excess electrons can flow through the conducting lead into Earth until the isolated imbalance is removed.
A positive metal sphere is earthed with no nearby charging object. Which way can electrons move?|From Earth to sphere.|Only from sphere to Earth.|Only from the insulating stand to the air.|Electrons cannot respond to a positive object.|The electron deficit can be filled from Earth through the conducting connection.
What is an electrical discharge?|Transfer of charge that reduces or redistributes an imbalance.|Creation of charge from nothing.|A proton losing its mass.|A continuous current in every object forever.|A discharge transfers existing charge. A spark is one visible form of discharge through air.
Why is the metal sphere mounted on an insulating stand in the model?|To stop an unintended conducting path to the surroundings.|To provide a second metal wire to Earth.|To make the sphere have no atoms.|To increase the number of protons by rubbing.|The stand helps the isolated sphere retain charge until the intended lead is attached.
A sufficiently strong electric field can make air do what?|Conduct, allowing a spark.|Become a perfect permanent insulator.|Remove all electrical forces.|Turn every electron into light with no charge left.|Electrical breakdown can make air conduct. Charge transfer through the conducting region can produce light, sound and heating.
What does bonding conducting fuel-handling parts aim to reduce?|Potential differences and the risk of a spark between them.|The number of nuclei in the fuel.|The Earth’s size.|All chemical energy in the fuel.|Bonding provides an electrical link between conducting parts; engineered procedures reduce spark risk. This is not a fuel-handling activity for students.
How does bonding differ from earthing?|Bonding joins conducting objects; earthing connects to Earth.|Bonding creates electrons; earthing creates protons.|They are names for opposite charge signs.|Bonding always uses an insulator.|The purposes can overlap in systems, but an inter-object connection and a connection to Earth are distinct ideas.
A selected sphere has 8 positive cores and 11 electrons. After ideal earthing without external influences, how many selected electrons balance those cores?|8.|11.|3.|0.|Three excess electrons leave in the simplified selected-patch model, leaving 8 positives and 8 negatives.
Why is a shortest-path-only rule insufficient to predict lightning?|Air breakdown and electric-field conditions determine a complex route.|Lightning can flow only through plastic.|Lightning never occurs inside clouds.|Charge cannot move through ionised air.|Lightning paths are more complex than a geometric shortest line; it may occur within clouds as well as to ground.''',
'Static electricity in everyday life':'''What helps a photocopier place toner on selected regions?|Controlled electrical attraction.|Using up electrons as ink.|Converting protons into paper.|Making the entire page a magnet only.|Toner particles respond to controlled charge patterns, then the image is transferred and fixed.
What can electrostatic spray painting use?|Attraction of charged droplets to a suitably connected workpiece.|Neutrons leaving every paint molecule.|Gravity being switched off.|A continuous supply of new charge created from nothing.|Electrostatic forces can assist deposition, while repulsion between similarly charged droplets can help spread a spray.
What is the main target of an electrostatic precipitator?|Suspended particles in a gas stream.|Every gas molecule without exception.|All heat in the chimney.|The whole building’s protons.|Charged particles are attracted towards collecting surfaces. This does not guarantee removal of gaseous pollutants.
Which is an everyday example of electrostatic discharge?|A brief spark after walking on carpet and touching metal.|A book simply resting on a table.|Water freezing in a tray.|A wooden ruler measuring a length.|Contact and separation can charge a person; touching a conductor can allow a brief discharge.
What makes a charged dust collector useful?|Electrical forces can direct charged particles towards a collection surface.|It creates dust from electrons.|It makes dust mass disappear.|It blocks all gases by turning them into protons.|The charge helps separate suspended material from flowing gas, but collection and later removal are still needed.
Which statement about a defibrillator is most accurate?|It delivers a controlled electrical pulse for certain abnormal heart rhythms.|It is simply a charged comb attracting the heart.|It restarts every heart regardless of rhythm.|It removes all electrons from a patient.|A defibrillator concerns stored electrical energy and a controlled current pulse; it is not merely electrostatic attraction or a universal restart device.
Why can clothing cling after drying?|Contact and separation can produce charge imbalances and attraction.|Fabric atoms have lost all nuclei.|Every item has exactly the same net charge and must attract.|Only gravity can act between fabrics.|Dry insulating materials can retain charge. Attraction may involve opposite charges or polarisation.
What keeps the pizza analogy distinct from static attraction?|The circuit model represents a continuing current in a closed loop.|Static attraction requires a steady closed metal loop.|The pizza model has no energy transfer.|Static charge has no electric forces.|Static examples concern a persisting imbalance; a driven circuit can sustain current and energy transfer.
Why should an industrial particle filter not be described as removing all pollution?|It targets particles and does not necessarily capture gaseous pollutants.|It cannot attract any charged matter.|Only neutral gases have mass.|All pollution consists of electrons.|Particles and gaseous pollutants are different. A particular separation method addresses specific materials.
Which statement connects static and circuit electricity correctly?|Both involve charge, but their arrangements and patterns of movement differ.|Static electricity contains only protons; circuits only neutrons.|Charge exists only when a switch is closed.|Circuit charge is always created by the battery.|The same charge concepts underlie both. A battery supplies energy and maintains conditions for flow; rubbing can redistribute existing charge.'''}
CIRCUITS={
'Current and charge flow':'''What does electric current measure?|Rate of flow of charge.|Energy per coulomb.|Total resistance of a wire.|Number of neutrons in a cell.|Current is charge transferred per time: I = Q/t. Its unit is the ampere, A.
What does a current of 1 A mean?|1 C passes a point each second.|1 J is stored in every electron.|1 Ω passes a point each second.|1 V is used up every second.|One ampere is one coulomb per second: 1 A = 1 C/s.
What does closing a switch do in a simple battery loop?|Completes the conducting path.|Creates all the wire’s electrons.|Removes the cell’s positive terminal.|Uses up every charged particle.|A complete loop permits sustained current. Mobile charges were present throughout the metal before closure.
In a steady series circuit, how does current before a resistor compare with current after it?|They are equal.|The current after is always zero.|The current before is twice the current after.|The resistor creates extra charge, so current after is greater.|Charge does not accumulate indefinitely in a steady series loop. Energy transfers, while the same charge rate continues.
6 C passes a point in 3 s. What is the current?|2 A.|18 A.|0.5 A.|9 A.|I = Q/t = 6 C ÷ 3 s = 2 C/s = 2 A.
A steady current is 0.5 A for 8 s. How much charge passes?|4 C.|16 C.|0.0625 C.|8.5 C.|Q = It = 0.5 A × 8 s = 4 C. The circuit does not use up this charge.
Which direction is conventional current in the external circuit?|From positive towards negative.|From negative towards positive by definition.|Only from Earth to a lamp.|Only towards the cell’s centre from both sides.|Conventional current follows positive-charge flow. Metal electrons drift in the opposite direction.
Which carriers drift through ordinary metal wires?|Electrons.|Positive ion cores flowing freely around the loop.|Neutrons only.|Whole battery cells.|Mobile electrons carry the net drift current; positive ion cores remain in the lattice.
At a junction, 0.7 A arrives and one branch takes 0.2 A. What current takes the other branch?|0.5 A.|0.9 A.|0.14 A.|3.5 A.|In a steady junction, entering current equals leaving current. The remaining branch current is 0.7 − 0.2 = 0.5 A.
Why can a lamp respond quickly when electrons drift slowly?|An electrical signal propagates through a circuit with charges already present.|One electron must complete the whole loop before the lamp responds.|The lamp creates electrons from light first.|Electrons drift at the speed of light in every metal.|Individual drift and electrical signal propagation are different. The current involves mobile charges already present around the circuit.''',
'Potential difference and energy':'''What is potential difference?|Energy transferred per unit charge between two points.|Charge transferred per second.|Resistance multiplied by time.|The number of electrons stored in a wire.|V = E/Q. Voltage compares two points and is measured in joules per coulomb, or volts.
What does 1 V mean?|1 J per coulomb.|1 C per second.|1 Ω per second.|1 W per metre.|The volt is a joule per coulomb: 1 V = 1 J/C. It is not itself a flow rate.
In the pizza model, what does the shop represent?|The cell supplying energy.|A resistor using up charge.|An ammeter measuring food.|The Earth removing all couriers.|The shop supplies pizzas as the cell supplies energy per charge. Couriers represent charge packets.
In the pizza model, what happens to couriers after the party?|They return to the shop without the delivered energy badge.|They are destroyed as the lamp uses charge.|They become new cell nuclei.|They remain permanently at the party.|Energy transfers at the component; charge continues around a complete loop.
A component transfers 12 J when 2 C passes. What is its potential difference?|6 V.|24 V.|0.167 V.|14 V.|V = E/Q = 12 J ÷ 2 C = 6 V.
Across a 3 V component, 4 C passes. How much energy transfers?|12 J.|0.75 J.|7 J.|1.33 J.|E = VQ = 3 V × 4 C = 12 J.
Why is voltage not the same thing as current?|Voltage is energy per charge; current is charge per time.|Both mean charge per time in different colours.|Voltage counts only protons and current only neutrons.|Voltage is always zero when current is non-zero.|Potential difference and charge-flow rate are distinct physical quantities with different units.
In a two-resistor series network, the supply is 6 V and one resistor has 2 V. What is the other potential difference?|4 V.|8 V.|12 V.|3 V.|Series potential differences add to the supply voltage, neglecting wire and internal resistance: 6 − 2 = 4 V.
At fixed 3 Ω resistance, the supply changes from 3 V to 6 V. How do current and energy per coulomb change?|Both double.|Only energy per coulomb doubles; current is unchanged.|Current halves while energy per coulomb doubles.|Both halve.|I = V/R doubles when V doubles at fixed R. Energy per coulomb is V and doubles too.
What is an important limit of the pizza model?|Real circuit energy transfer involves electromagnetic fields, not literal parcels carried on electrons.|A cell cannot supply energy.|Real lamps must consume charge.|There is no complete path in any real circuit.|The analogy separates charge circulation from energy transfer. It does not describe the full electromagnetic mechanism.''',
'Resistance and Ohm’s law':'''What unit measures resistance?|Ohm, Ω.|Ampere, A.|Coulomb, C.|Watt, W.|Resistance is measured in ohms. A, C and W measure current, charge and power respectively.
Which expression gives current from voltage and resistance?|I = V/R.|I = VR.|I = R/V.|I = V + R.|Rearrange V = IR by dividing both sides by R. Use volts and ohms to obtain amps.
For an ohmic resistor, when does current stay proportional to voltage?|When temperature and other physical conditions stay constant.|Only when its charge is used up.|Whenever temperature changes without limit.|Only when it is an insulator with zero current.|Ohm’s law describes a constant-resistance relationship under unchanged physical conditions.
At fixed voltage, what happens when resistance increases?|Current decreases.|Current always increases.|Current stays identical in every circuit.|Charge begins to be destroyed.|I = V/R. Increasing the denominator at fixed V reduces the steady current.
A 20 Ω resistor has 6 V across it. What current flows?|0.30 A.|120 A.|3.33 A.|26 A.|I = V/R = 6/20 = 0.30 A.
A resistor has 4 V across it and 0.20 A through it. What is its resistance?|20 Ω.|0.8 Ω.|0.05 Ω.|4.2 Ω.|R = V/I = 4/0.20 = 20 Ω. Divide by 0.20, rather than multiply.
At fixed resistance, what does doubling voltage do to current?|Doubles it.|Halves it.|Leaves it unchanged.|Makes it four times as large.|Direct proportion means the same multiplicative change in V and I when R is constant.
Why can an incandescent filament lamp have a curved I–V graph?|Its temperature and resistance rise as current heats it.|It uses up more electrons at higher voltage.|It has no charge carriers.|Every conductor must have a straight I–V graph.|Heating changes the filament’s resistance, so it is not a constant-temperature ohmic resistor across its full range.
Why does R = V/I at one operating point not prove Ohm’s law?|Many components have a ratio at one point without a constant ratio across voltages.|The ratio cannot ever be calculated.|Every component has identical resistance.|Ohm’s law requires current to decrease when voltage increases.|An ohmic test checks proportionality over several readings with temperature and conditions controlled.
Why should R not be obtained from 0 V divided by 0 A?|0/0 is undefined; use a non-zero point or the line gradient.|Every resistor has zero resistance at zero voltage.|Zero current proves infinite resistance for every component.|The meter always measures protons at zero voltage.|The ratio at the origin is indeterminate. A constant-resistance line’s non-zero points or gradient provide the resistance.''',
'Meters and current–voltage graphs':'''How should an ammeter be connected?|In series with the measured path.|Directly across the supply without a load.|In place of both supply terminals at once.|Outside the circuit with no connection.|An ammeter measures current through a path. Its very low resistance makes a direct supply connection inappropriate.
How should a voltmeter be connected?|In parallel across the component or network.|Only in series as the sole path.|Inside the battery’s chemical material.|To one unconnected wire only.|A voltmeter compares two points across the measured component and has high resistance.
On our I–V graph, what is on the horizontal axis?|Potential difference in volts.|Current in amps.|Resistance in ohms.|Power in watts.|Our graph plots I vertically against V horizontally. Axis orientation matters when interpreting gradient.
What graph represents an ohmic resistor at constant temperature?|A straight I–V line through the origin.|A horizontal line at non-zero current for all voltages.|A circle centred on the origin.|An arbitrary curve whose slope changes continuously.|Direct proportion means a straight line through (0,0) for I versus V.
For I vertically and V horizontally, what does the gradient equal?|1/R.|R.|VI.|The total number of electrons.|Gradient is ΔI/ΔV. Since I = V/R, it equals reciprocal resistance, in A/V.
Which I–V line means a lower resistance under these axis conventions?|The steeper line.|The shallower line.|Any line with a higher maximum x-axis label.|Only a curved line.|At a given voltage, lower resistance permits greater current, so I/V and the graph gradient are larger.
Why should one I–V dataset keep the same resistor and temperature?|To test voltage–current proportionality for fixed physical conditions.|To make charge disappear at the same rate.|Because voltage is not allowed to change.|To avoid needing an ammeter.|Changing resistor or temperature changes resistance and creates a different relationship, not another point on the same controlled test.
If V is plotted vertically against I horizontally, what is the ohmic gradient?|R.|1/R.|Q/t.|Always 1 regardless of resistor.|With V = RI, the gradient ΔV/ΔI equals resistance. Always read the axis labels before interpreting slope.
A graph rises 0.20 A over a 4 V horizontal change. What resistance does it indicate?|20 Ω.|0.05 Ω.|0.8 Ω.|4.2 Ω.|The I–V gradient is 0.20/4 = 0.05 A/V = 1/R, so R = 1/0.05 = 20 Ω.
Why does our ideal voltmeter not materially change branch current?|It is modelled with infinite resistance and draws no current.|It has zero resistance and short-circuits the branch.|It creates exactly the same charge it uses.|It connects to only one point.|An ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance. Real voltmeters have high, finite resistance, which can affect sensitive circuits.''',
'Series and parallel circuits':'''What describes two resistors in series?|They are in one path, one after the other.|They are connected across the same two junctions.|They must have equal resistance.|Neither is connected to the supply.|Series means a single sequential path, so steady current is the same through both components.
What describes two resistors in parallel?|They connect across the same two junctions.|They always carry exactly equal currents.|Their resistances must be added directly.|Only one can be connected at a time.|Parallel branches share the same potential difference; their currents depend on their resistances.
What is the total resistance of 20 Ω and 40 Ω in series?|60 Ω.|13.33 Ω.|800 Ω.|2 Ω.|For series resistors, R_total = R₁ + R₂ = 20 + 40 = 60 Ω.
Which quantity is the same across ideal parallel branches?|Potential difference.|Current in every unequal branch.|Resistance of every branch.|Number of components in each branch.|Each branch connects across the same pair of junctions and therefore has the same voltage.
A 6 V supply drives 20 Ω and 40 Ω in series. What is the current?|0.10 A.|0.30 A.|0.45 A.|10 A.|The total resistance is 60 Ω, so I = 6/60 = 0.10 A through both resistors.
At 6 V, how much current flows in a 40 Ω parallel branch?|0.15 A.|0.30 A.|0.45 A.|240 A.|That branch has the full 6 V, so I₂ = 6/40 = 0.15 A.
At 6 V, 20 Ω and 40 Ω are in parallel. What is supply current?|0.45 A.|0.10 A.|0.15 A.|0.30 A.|Branch currents are 6/20 = 0.30 A and 6/40 = 0.15 A. Their sum is 0.45 A.
What happens to equivalent resistance when an extra finite-resistance parallel branch is added?|It decreases.|It always increases.|It is unchanged for every new branch.|It becomes the sum of all branch resistances.|The extra branch provides another conducting path. For positive branch resistances, equivalent resistance is lower than before.
For 20 Ω and 40 Ω in parallel, which total resistance is correct?|Approximately 13.33 Ω.|60 Ω.|30 Ω.|800 Ω.|R_total = R₁R₂/(R₁ + R₂) = 800/60 ≈ 13.33 Ω, less than either branch resistance.
In a series circuit, current is 0.10 A through 20 Ω and 40 Ω. What are the voltage drops?|2 V and 4 V.|4 V and 2 V.|6 V and 6 V.|0.2 V and 0.4 V.|V₁ = IR₁ = 0.10 × 20 = 2 V; V₂ = 0.10 × 40 = 4 V. The larger resistance takes the larger share of voltage.''',
'Wire factors, power and applications':'''Which wire factor describes the material itself at a stated temperature?|Resistivity, ρ.|Length, L.|Cross-sectional area, A.|Circuit time, t.|Resistivity is a material property in Ω m. Resistance also depends on geometry: R = ρL/A.
Keeping material, area and temperature fixed, what does doubling wire length do?|Doubles resistance.|Halves resistance.|Divides resistance by four.|Leaves resistance unchanged.|R is directly proportional to L for a uniform wire at fixed resistivity and area.
Keeping other factors fixed, what does increasing wire cross-sectional area do?|Lowers resistance.|Raises resistance.|Uses up electrons.|Changes all electrons to positive particles.|R = ρL/A. A larger conducting area provides more parallel paths and lowers resistance.
What is electrical power?|Rate of energy transfer.|Charge stored per metre.|Total energy regardless of time.|Voltage divided by charge.|P = E/t, measured in watts, where 1 W = 1 J/s. For a circuit component, P = VI.
A 6 V component carries 0.30 A. What is its power?|1.8 W.|20 W.|0.05 W.|6.3 W.|P = VI = 6 × 0.30 = 1.8 W.
A component transfers energy at 2 W for 10 s. How much energy transfers?|20 J.|0.2 J.|5 J.|12 J.|E = Pt = 2 W × 10 s = 20 J.
For our ordinary metal models, what usually happens when temperature increases?|Resistance increases because lattice vibrations increase scattering.|Positive ion cores flow around the loop.|Resistance must become exactly zero.|Every material has an identical temperature response.|These approximate metal models have positive temperature coefficients. Other materials and temperature ranges can behave differently.
Keeping material, length and temperature fixed, what does doubling a round wire’s diameter do to resistance?|Divides it by four.|Doubles it.|Halves it.|Multiplies it by four.|Area A = π(d/2)² grows by a factor of four, so R = ρL/A falls to one quarter.
At fixed transmitted power, why can higher transmission voltage reduce cable losses?|It allows lower current, reducing I²R heating without needing a change in cable resistance.|Voltage itself makes the cable’s resistivity zero.|It removes the need for a complete circuit.|It makes current and power identical quantities.|P = VI, so higher V permits lower I at the same P. Cable heating depends on I²R; the resistance need not decrease.
At fixed supply voltage, what happens to resistor power if resistance doubles?|Power halves.|Power doubles.|Power increases fourfold.|Power is unchanged.|At fixed V, P = V²/R, so doubling R halves total power. The statement P = I²R must be used with the actual changed current, not a falsely fixed I.'''}
for unit,rows in [('static',STATIC),('circuits',CIRCUITS)]:
 bank=dict(topicId=f'y9-electricity-{unit}',title='Static electricity and charge' if unit=='static' else 'Electric circuits',categories=list(rows),questions=[])
 for ci,(category,block) in enumerate(rows.items()):
  lines=block.strip().splitlines();assert len(lines)==10,(category,len(lines))
  for j,line in enumerate(lines):
   prompt,correct,a,b,c,explanation=line.split('|');pos=len(bank['questions'])%4;options=[a,b,c];options.insert(pos,correct)
   bank['questions'].append(dict(id=f'y9-electricity-{unit}-{ci+1}{j:02}',category=category,difficulty='Foundation' if j<4 else 'Application' if j<8 else 'Challenge',prompt=prompt,options=options,answer=pos,explanation=explanation))
 (ROOT/f'assets/revision/questions/y9-electricity-{unit}.json').write_text(json.dumps(bank,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
 if unit=='static':static=bank
 else:circuits=bank
checks={'static':[static['questions'][i] for i in [14,24,42,58]],'pizza':[circuits['questions'][i] for i in [12,13,17,19]],'resistance':[circuits['questions'][i] for i in [3,23,26,59]],'ohms-law':[circuits['questions'][i] for i in [30,31,34,46]],'wire-resistance':[circuits['questions'][i] for i in [50,51,56,57]]}
(ROOT/'assets/y9-electricity/checks.mjs').write_text('// Generated formative checks. No identity collection or recording.\nexport const CHECKS='+json.dumps(checks,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+';\n')
